WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.324
Smart pointers encode ownership patterns.

2
00:00:02.404 --> 00:00:04.523
`Box<T>` gives one owner for
heap-allocated data.

3
00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:06.771
It is still single ownership: the box owns
the value,

4
00:00:06.851 --> 00:00:08.675
and cleanup happens when the box is
dropped.

5
00:00:08.755 --> 00:00:11.959
This is useful when indirection is needed
without creating multiple cleanup

6
00:00:12.039 --> 00:00:12.630
authorities.

7
00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.815
`Rc<T>` supports coordinated shared
ownership in single-threaded code.

8
00:00:15.895 --> 00:00:18.655
`Arc<T>` supports coordinated shared
ownership across threads.

9
00:00:18.735 --> 00:00:21.545
These types allow multiple strong owners
to point to the same value,

10
00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:23.695
but they do not create independent cleanup
paths.

11
00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:26.387
The value is dropped only when the final
strong owner goes away.

12
00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:27.551
That difference matters.

13
00:00:27.631 --> 00:00:29.553
Shared ownership is not duplicate
ownership.

14
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:33.182
Duplicate ownership means more than one
path incorrectly believes it independently

15
00:00:33.262 --> 00:00:34.395
owns the same raw resource.

16
00:00:34.475 --> 00:00:38.123
Coordinated shared ownership uses
reference counting so cleanup happens exactly once

17
00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:38.942
at the right time.

18
00:00:39.022 --> 00:00:41.043
`Weak` references represent non-owning
links.

19
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:43.045
A weak reference does not keep the value
alive.

20
00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:45.737
Code must check whether the value still
exists before using it.

21
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:48.873
This is useful for parent-child
relationships, caches, observer lists,

22
00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:52.649
graph-like structures, and
lifecycle-sensitive designs where not every relationship

23
00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:54.011
should extend cleanup timing.

24
00:00:54.091 --> 00:00:56.456
Defensive design chooses smart pointers
deliberately.

25
00:00:56.536 --> 00:00:58.311
Shared ownership can solve real problems,

26
00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.510
but it can also make lifecycle timing less
obvious.

27
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.646
Review whether sharing is truly needed,
whether mutation is controlled,

28
00:01:03.726 --> 00:01:04.958
whether cycles are possible,

29
00:01:05.038 --> 00:01:08.340
and whether a weaker non-owning
relationship would better express the design.
